                        <cell role="num">112,368,000</cell>
                    </row>
                </table>
                <p>(See Report of Silver Committee, Appendix, p. 1.)</p>
                <p>The amount of gold <hi rendition="#italic">now</hi> in circulation is probably
                    £133,000,000.</p>
                <p>In 1860 the amount of silver in circulation was estimated at £14,799,000. In 1875
                        <pb facs="0530" n="434" xml:id="pg434"/> (December 31) the amount of silver
                    in circulation was estimated at £19,536,000. (See Report of Silver Committee,
                    Appendix, p. 1.)</p>
                <p>The amount of silver <hi rendition="#italic">now</hi> in circulation is probably
                    £20,000,000.</p>
                <p>The amount of paper money in circulation is about £46,000,000. (See Statistical
                    Abstract, pp. 119–121.)</p>
                <p>Previous to 1858 there was no registry kept at the custom-house of the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">imports</hi> of gold and silver, only of the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">exports</hi>. The subjoined tables will, therefore, date
                    from that year.</p>
