                <p>The <hi rendition="#italic">gold</hi> sent to the mint is coined, without charge,
                    into sovereigns and half-sovereigns, at the rate of £3 17<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >s</hi>. 10½<hi rendition="#italic">d</hi>. per ounce. Practically, however,
                    the bank of England is the mint’s only customer. The bank by its charter is
                    obliged to purchase the gold at £3 17<hi rendition="#italic">s</hi>. 9<hi
                        rendition="#italic">d</hi>. per ounce; and importers prefer to so dispose of
                    it rather than await the time occupied in the process of coinage. When there
                    happens to be a simultaneous <hi rendition="#italic">export</hi> demand for <hi
                        rendition="#italic">gold</hi> bullion with the <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >imports, gold</hi> in the open market commands a fraction over £3 17<hi
                        rendition="#italic">s</hi>. 9<hi rendition="#italic">d</hi>. per ounce. The
                    gold coinage of the kingdom is supplemented by Australian sovereigns. <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Silver</hi> is coined solely on behalf of the government
                    at large annual profits, notwithstanding it redeems the worn silver coins at
                    par. Part of the profit on <hi rendition="#italic">silver</hi> is lost by the
                    expense and waste connected with the gold coinage.</p>
                <p rendition="#center"><hi rendition="#small-caps">gold.</hi></p>
